\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Embedding-dependent nearest-neighbour statistics in two-dimensional binary prime patterns}

\author[put]{Dawid Kucharski\corref{cor1}}
\ead{dawid.kucharski@put.poznan.pl}
\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author}
\affiliation[put]{organisation={Poznan University of Technology},
            city={Poznan},
            country={Poland}}

\begin{abstract}
Binary two-dimensional embeddings of the prime sequence are studied under three
deterministic mappings: row-major order, the Ulam spiral, and Cantor diagonal
pairing. The three mappings act on one common integer population, the $9\,580$
primes below $99\,856$, placed in a $316\times316$ observation window. A local
spacing descriptor obtained by maximum-likelihood fitting of the Brody
distribution to unit-mean nearest-neighbour spacings is compared with the
Clark--Evans index, Ripley's $K$ function, exact-distance pair correlations,
and box-counting and pairwise estimates of the generalised dimension $D_2$.
The fitted Brody parameter is found to be strongly embedding-dependent:
$2.153$ (95\% CI $[2.086,2.224]$) for row-major order, $1.856$ ($[1.811,1.904]$)
for the Ulam spiral, and $1.413$ ($[1.387,1.440]$) for Cantor pairing, against
$1.466\pm0.021$ for the density-matched lattice Bernoulli null over $1\,000$
realisations, none of which exceeds $\beta=1.53$. The ordering survives all
boundary treatments tested, and it is not reproduced by an inhomogeneous
Bernoulli null calibrated to the prime-number-theorem density. Exact-distance
pair correlations of the row-major embedding equal $1.337\pm0.030$ at lattice
distance $2$ in the horizontal direction, in agreement with the
Hardy--Littlewood twin-prime constant $2C_2\approx1.3203$; the measured
exact-distance pair counts of all three embeddings agree with the
Hardy--Littlewood pair densities of the integer gap classes that each
embedding maps to the corresponding lattice distance. The fitted parameter is
insensitive to removal of all twin and cousin primes, so it is not a proxy
for those pair excesses alone. None of the multiscale estimators examined
provides additional discrimination between embeddings at the scales tested.
It is concluded that the apparent local regularity of embedded
prime patterns is a joint property of the sequence and of the embedding, not
of the sequence alone.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
prime numbers \sep spatial embeddings \sep nearest-neighbour statistics \sep
Brody distribution \sep point processes
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

%=====================================================================
\section{Introduction}
%=====================================================================

Two-dimensional visualisations of arithmetic sequences have long been used as
heuristic tools for detecting hidden regularities. The best known example is
the Ulam spiral, where primes cluster along visually prominent diagonals that
are absent in a random arrangement of occupied sites~\cite{ULAM-SPIRAL-1963}.
Such constructions raise a natural statistical question: when a one-dimensional
arithmetic sequence is embedded in two dimensions, which apparent spatial
features belong to the sequence itself, and which are created by the embedding?

This question is harder than it first appears because different descriptors
probe different spatial scales. Local spacing summaries focus on short-range
adjacency, while multiscale descriptors respond to mass distribution over many
box sizes simultaneously. A sequence may therefore appear highly structured
under one descriptor and only weakly structured under another. The prime
sequence provides a useful benchmark because it is deterministic, sparse, and
subject to well-known local arithmetic constraints such as the impossibility of
two consecutive integers both being prime for $n>2$~\cite{HARDY-WRIGHT}.

The Brody distribution,
\begin{equation}
P_\beta(s)=(\beta+1)as^\beta e^{-as^{\beta+1}},
\qquad
a=\left[\Gamma\!\left(\frac{\beta+2}{\beta+1}\right)\right]^{\beta+1},
\label{eq:brody}
\end{equation}
was introduced in quantum chaos as a one-parameter interpolation between the
Poisson and Wigner limits of one-dimensional level-spacing
statistics~\cite{BRODY-1981}.

The prime sequence itself has been examined with spacing statistics borrowed
from random-matrix theory. In an analysis by Wolf of the
nearest-neighbour-spacing distribution of primes, Poisson-type statistics were
reported after suitable rescaling, which indicates pseudo-random behaviour at
the smallest scales~\cite{WOLF-2014}. In a complementary study, the spacings
between adjacent primes were investigated within classical and
superstatistical random-matrix theory and were described as a mixture of
regular and chaotic components~\cite{ABDELMAGEED-2021}. The tension between
these two descriptions suggests that the local statistics attributed to the
prime sequence depend on the representation, the rescaling, and the fitting
model, a sensitivity that is made quantitative in the present work.

Brody-type fits have since been used as a convenient empirical summary of
local spacing regularity in several fields. The crossover between regular and
chaotic level statistics in quantum-dot spectroscopy was described by
Brody-type fits~\cite{ALHASSID-2000}. Applications of spacing statistics to
wave-chaotic systems are reviewed in the monograph of
St\"ockmann~\cite{STOCKMANN-1999}. In elastodynamics, spectral level spacings
were fitted by Brody-type distributions to quantify departures from
random-matrix predictions~\cite{WEAVER-1989}. Brody-type fits were also applied
to eigenvalue spacings of financial correlation
matrices~\cite{LALOUX-1999}. For spatial point patterns, it was shown by Sakhr
and Nieminen that nearest-neighbour spacing statistics of random points on
fractals can be described by Brody-type laws, thereby linking RMT-inspired
spacing families to spatial data~\cite{SAKHR-NIEMINEN-2005}. In the present
paper, $\beta$ is used only as an \emph{empirical fit parameter} for local
nearest-neighbour spacings, not as a universal exclusion scale and not as
evidence of any random-matrix symmetry class.

A separate methodological tradition concerns spatial point patterns, for which
distance-based summaries are standard. The nearest-neighbour index as a
measure of departure from complete spatial randomness was introduced by Clark
and Evans~\cite{CLARK-EVANS-1954}. The classical account of second-order
summaries, centred on the $K$ function, was given by
Ripley~\cite{RIPLEY-1981}. A systematic treatment of summary statistics and
their edge corrections is provided in the monograph of Illian and
co-workers~\cite{ILLIAN-2008}, and model-based inference for point processes is
developed by Diggle~\cite{DIGGLE-2013}. Practical implementations of these
diagnostics are available in the spatstat software~\cite{BADDELEY-TURNER-2005}.
The choice of null model is not innocuous in this setting: it was shown by
Roxburgh and Matsuki that null models must be validated against the
autocorrelation structure of the data, because an inappropriate null can
distort the type I error rate~\cite{ROXBURGH-MATSUKI-1999}. This caution
applies directly to the Bernoulli nulls used in the present work, which are
therefore always density-matched to the prime surface.

At larger scales, fractal geometry provides the standard vocabulary for
mass-distribution structure. Fractional dimension as a statistical descriptor
was introduced by Mandelbrot~\cite{MANDELBROT-1967}, and fractal
characterisations of spatial phenomena were surveyed by Goodchild and
Mark~\cite{GOODCHILD-MARK-1987}. In nonlinear time-series analysis,
correlation-dimension estimates separate low-dimensional deterministic
dynamics from randomness; they must, however, be interpreted with caution:
estimates can saturate for chaotic series, grow without bound for stochastic
series, and be distorted by limited sample
size~\cite{ISLAM-SIVAKUMAR-2002}. Related nearest-neighbour embedding
diagnostics can misclassify stochastic data unless carefully
modified~\cite{FREDKIN-RICE-1995}, and a statistically reliable
nearest-neighbour estimator of the embedding dimension was proposed by
Chelidze~\cite{CHELIDZE-2017}. Finite-sample corrections for dimension
estimators were analysed by Theiler~\cite{THEILER-1986}.

For unit-mean nearest-neighbour spacings under complete spatial randomness in
two dimensions, the analytic Rayleigh law,
\begin{equation}
P_{\rm CSR}(s)=\frac{\pi s}{2}\exp\!\left(-\frac{\pi s^2}{4}\right),
\label{eq:rayleigh}
\end{equation}
coincides algebraically with the Brody family at $\beta=1$. The continuous CSR
law is, however, not the empirical null for binary fields on a lattice: the
density-matched lattice Bernoulli null fitted with the same procedure below
yields $\beta\approx1.47$ at the native density, because lattice discreteness
modifies the spacing distribution. Both reference levels are therefore
reported separately in all figures and tables.

These strands have developed largely independently. Prime-gap statistics treat
the sequence without reference to a two-dimensional geometry; Brody
nearest-neighbour results for fractals concern random point sets rather than
deterministic arithmetic patterns; distance-based point-pattern methodology
has been developed mainly for natural and experimental patterns; and
correlation-dimension diagnostics have been applied mainly to time series. No
single study compares a local Brody-type nearest-neighbour fit with multiscale
descriptors under a validated, density-matched Bernoulli null across several
deterministic two-dimensional embeddings of one arithmetic sequence. To the
best of the present knowledge, the
research gap addressed by the present manuscript is therefore the missing
comparison between descriptor classes for embedded deterministic sequences:
whether the apparent spatial structure of an embedded prime sequence is a
property of the sequence, of the embedding, or of the descriptor.

The question is addressed in this paper for binary 2D prime patterns. Three
deterministic embeddings are compared on one common integer population and one
common observation window: row-major order, the Ulam spiral, and Cantor
pairing. Three classes of controls are used. First, a matched-density
Bernoulli null separates arithmetic structure from trivial occupancy effects.
Second, an inhomogeneous Bernoulli null calibrated to the
prime-number-theorem density separates arithmetic structure from the smooth
density gradient of the prime sequence. Third, a sparse-integer control and
density-thinning experiments test whether the row-major prime signal is merely
a consequence of low fill fraction. In parallel, multiscale structure is
quantified through box-counting partition statistics and validated by the
classical Grassberger--Procaccia pairwise correlation sum. The central result
is that local spacing fits are strongly embedding-dependent, whereas none of
the multiscale estimators examined provides a comparably robust
discrimination. The same prime sequence therefore does not have a unique 2D
spatial signature independent of how it is embedded and how it is measured.

%=====================================================================
\section{Methods}
%=====================================================================

\subsection{Prime population and observation window}

One common integer population is used throughout. Let $W=316$ and
$N_{\rm pop}=W^2=99\,856$. The prime population consists of all primes
$p\le 99\,856$; there are $9\,580$ of them, giving a fill fraction
$\rho=9\,580/99\,856=0.09594$. The twelve primes in $(99\,856,10^5]$ are
excluded so that all three embeddings act on the same finite set in the same
square observation window $\{0,\dots,315\}^2$, and every descriptor is computed
for exactly this population.

\subsection{Deterministic embeddings}

All coordinates are zero-indexed; the metric is Euclidean distance between
cell centres.

\begin{definition}[Row-major embedding]
The integer $n\in\{1,\dots,N_{\rm pop}\}$ is mapped to cell
$(i,j)=(\lfloor(n-1)/W\rfloor,(n-1)\bmod W)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Ulam spiral on an even side]
The spiral is generated on the $316\times316$ lattice with start cell
$(x_0,y_0)=(157,158)$ and directions east, north, west, south with step
lengths $1,1,2,2,3,3,\dots$; the $n$-th cell visited receives the integer $n$.
This procedure is a bijection of $\{1,\dots,99\,856\}$ onto
$\{0,\dots,315\}^2$ (verified computationally).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cantor diagonal sweep with row wrap]
For $s=0,1,\dots,2W-2$, the cells $(i,j)$ with $i+j=s$ are visited in
increasing order of $j$, where $j$ runs from $j_0=\max(0,s-W+1)$ to
$j_1=\min(s,W-1)$ and $i=s-j$; the $n$-th visited cell receives the integer
$n$. Each cell of $\{0,\dots,W-1\}^2$ is visited exactly once (verified
computationally), so this map is a bijection of $\{1,\dots,W^2\}$ onto the
window. Consecutive integers occupy diagonal neighbours $(i,j)\to(i-1,j+1)$
within each diagonal, and the last cell of each diagonal is connected to the
first cell of the next diagonal by a long jump whose length grows with $s$.
\end{definition}

The three mappings therefore differ only in which arithmetic neighbours
become lattice neighbours. Row-major maps consecutive integers to horizontally
adjacent cells; the Ulam spiral maps consecutive integers to adjacent cells
along the spiral arm; the Cantor sweep maps consecutive integers to diagonally
adjacent cells within each diagonal block, with long jumps at the block
boundaries.

\subsection{Nearest-neighbour descriptor}

Occupied cells form a point pattern with coordinates $(j,i)$ at pixel centres.
Euclidean nearest-neighbour distances are computed with a KD-tree, normalised
to unit mean, and fitted by maximum likelihood to the Brody family
of Eq.~\eqref{eq:brody}. Bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals are obtained from
$3\,000$ resamples of the spacing sample. Boundary sensitivity is assessed
with three treatments applied identically to prime patterns and null models:
(i) \emph{open} (plain Euclidean distances within the window); (ii)
\emph{toroidal} (distances under periodic boundary conditions, self-pairs
excluded); (iii) \emph{interior reference} (only points at distance $\ge5$ px
from the boundary are used as reference points, with Euclidean distances to
all points).

\subsection{Null models and controls}

Three null models are used. The \emph{matched-density Bernoulli null} occupies
each of the $99\,856$ cells independently with probability $\rho$. The
\emph{sparse-integer control} draws $9\,580$ distinct integers uniformly from
$\{1,\dots,99\,856\}$ and embeds them row-major. The \emph{inhomogeneous
Bernoulli null} occupies cell $n$ with probability $p(n)=c/\ln n$ for
$n\ge3$ and $p(1)=p(2)=0$, where $c$ is calibrated so that the expected number
of occupied cells equals $9\,580$; this null reproduces the smooth density
gradient implied by the prime number theorem without any local arithmetic
correlations. Null distributions are estimated from $1\,000$ independent
realisations per null model.

Density effects are assessed by random thinning of the row-major prime
surface to target fill fractions $\rho\in\{0.010,0.020,0.032,0.050,0.096\}$,
with $100$ thinning realisations per level, each compared with a
density-matched Bernoulli ensemble of $1\,000$ realisations.

\subsection{Standard spatial diagnostics}

The Clark--Evans nearest-neighbour index is $R=2\sqrt{\rho}\,\bar r$, where
$\bar r$ is the mean nearest-neighbour distance. A modern treatment of these
summary statistics and their estimation is collected in the monograph of
Baddeley, Rubak, and Turner~\cite{BADDELEY-2015}. Ripley's $K$ function is
estimated with the isotropic border correction (fraction of the circumference
inside the window, 64 angular samples) on 18 log-spaced radii
$r\in[1,W/3]$ pixels, and is summarised by $L(r)-r$ evaluated at the mean
nearest-neighbour scale and by its maximal absolute deviation. The pair
correlation function at an exact lattice distance $d$ is computed by cyclic
cross-correlation on the torus,
\begin{equation}
g_2(d)=\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{s}\in\mathcal{S}_d}\sum_{\mathbf{x}}
Z_{\mathbf{x}}Z_{\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{s}}}
{N\,\rho\,m_d/2},
\label{eq:g2exact}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{S}_d$ is a set of shift representatives of the lattice
directions at distance $d$, $m_d$ is the number of lattice directions at
distance $d$ on the torus ($m_d=4$ for $r=1,\sqrt2,2,4$ and $m_d=8$ for
$r=\sqrt5$), $N$ is the number of occupied cells, and the inner sum counts each
unordered pair exactly once; for the lattice Bernoulli null the expectation
of $g_2(d)$ is exactly $1$ for every $d$ (verified numerically, Section S1.3).
The continuous annulus version $g_2$ over $1\le r<3$ pixels is computed by
toroidal pair counting with $3\,000$ reference points.

Multiscale structure is quantified by the box-counting partition function of
order two,
\begin{equation}
Z_2(\varepsilon)=\sum_{k}\left(\frac{M_k(\varepsilon)}
{M(\varepsilon)}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $M_k(\varepsilon)$ are box masses and $M(\varepsilon)$ is the total mass
of the cropped region at box size $\varepsilon$; the slope of $\log Z_2$
against $\log\varepsilon$ over $\varepsilon\in\{2,4,\dots,128\}$ px is the
box-counting estimate of the generalised dimension $D_2$. Because finite-sample
box-counting and pairwise estimators are not interchangeable, the classical
Grassberger--Procaccia pairwise correlation sum
\begin{equation}
C(r)=\frac{2}{N(N-1)}\sum_{i<j}\Theta(r-d_{ij})
\end{equation}
is computed as an independent validation, and the slope of $\log C(r)$ against
$\log r$ over $r\in[2,105]$ px is reported separately.

%=====================================================================
\section{Results}
%=====================================================================

\subsection{Embedding dependence of the local spacing fit}

The fitted Brody parameter is strongly embedding-dependent
(Table~\ref{tab:local}). At the common window $316\times316$, row-major primes
yield $\beta=2.153$ ($[2.086,2.224]$), the Ulam spiral yields $\beta=1.856$
($[1.811,1.904]$), and Cantor pairing yields $\beta=1.413$
($[1.387,1.440]$). The density-matched Bernoulli null yields
$\beta=1.466\pm0.021$. The ordering is unchanged under all three boundary
treatments (Table~\ref{tab:local}). Toroidal boundary conditions give
$\beta=2.187$ ($[2.121,2.251]$) for row-major order, $1.890$
($[1.848,1.935]$) for the Ulam spiral, and $1.423$ ($[1.398,1.449]$) for
Cantor pairing. The interior-reference treatment gives $2.199$
($[2.133,2.267]$), $1.894$ ($[1.851,1.941]$), and $1.429$
($[1.402,1.455]$). Boundary effects therefore shift
the fitted values by at most a few hundredths but do not affect the
conclusions. As a distribution-free assessment, no null realisation among the
$1\,000$ Bernoulli replicas produced a fitted $\beta$ above $1.526$, whereas
the row-major value is $2.153$.
Figure~\ref{fig:embedding-robustness} compares the fitted values
with the null ensemble, and Figure~\ref{fig:boundary-sensitivity} shows the
boundary sensitivity explicitly.

