\subsection{Controls: sparsity, density gradient, and thinning}

The row-major excess is not a consequence of sparsity. The sparse-integer
control yields $\beta=1.465\pm0.020$, and the inhomogeneous Bernoulli null
calibrated to the prime-number-theorem density yields $\beta=1.451\pm0.020$;
both are far below the row-major value of $2.153$ and close to the
density-matched Bernoulli null. The smooth density gradient of the prime
sequence therefore does not explain the elevated local spacing regularity of
the row-major embedding.

Density-thinning changes the absolute value of $\beta$ substantially
(Figure~\ref{fig:density-thinning-control}). The thinned row-major primes give
$\beta=1.155\pm0.066$, $1.289\pm0.052$, $1.468\pm0.041$, $1.706\pm0.032$, and
$2.153$ (deterministic, no thinning) at $\rho=0.010$, $0.020$, $0.032$,
$0.050$, $0.096$, against
density-matched Bernoulli nulls of $1.030\pm0.063$, $1.091\pm0.043$,
$1.155\pm0.035$, $1.248\pm0.028$, and $1.467\pm0.021$. The prime surface
remains separated from the matched null at every tested density. The null
level itself grows with density and approaches the continuous CSR value as
the density decreases ($0.945\pm0.140$, $0.991\pm0.094$, $1.026\pm0.062$ at
$\rho=0.002$, $0.005$, $0.010$), consistently with the known lattice-CSR
behaviour of binary fields on discrete lattices~\cite{CHIU-2013}; this is why
the null is quoted empirically rather than by the continuous CSR value
$\beta=1$.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_density_thinning_control.pdf}
  \caption{Density dependence of the fitted Brody parameter for thinned
           row-major primes (100 realisations per level, 95\% band) and
           density-matched Bernoulli nulls (1000 realisations per level).
           The continuous CSR reference $\beta=1$ is marked for comparison.}
  \label{fig:density-thinning-control}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Exact-distance pair correlations}

Distance-resolved pair correlations expose the arithmetic content of the
local structure (Table~\ref{tab:g2}, Figure~\ref{fig:g2-distance}). In the
row-major embedding, integer gaps map to horizontal offsets, so lattice
distance $2$ corresponds to twin primes and lattice distance $4$ to cousin
primes. The measured direction-resolved values are $g_2(r=2)=1.337\pm0.030$
horizontally and $1.332\pm0.029$ vertically, and $g_2(r=4)=1.405\pm0.030$
horizontally and $1.339\pm0.030$ vertically, against a null expectation of
exactly $1$ in each direction. The horizontal value at $r=2$ equals
$\pi_2(x)/(\pi(x)^2/x)$ evaluated at $x=99\,856$ and is in agreement with the
Hardy--Littlewood twin-prime prediction $2C_2\approx1.3203$
\cite{HARDY-LITTLEWOOD-1923}. The vertical values correspond to the prime gaps
$632$ and $1264$, whose Hardy--Littlewood densities carry the same factor
$2C_2$ (multiplied by $\prod_{p\mid g}(p-1)/(p-2)=78/77\approx1.013$ for the
factor $79$ of the window side), consistent with the measured values.

This identification is not merely qualitative. For every embedding and every
lattice distance tested, the measured pair count was compared with the sum of
the exact Hardy--Littlewood pair densities $\pi_g(x)$ of the integer gap
classes $g$ that the embedding maps to that lattice distance (computed by
sieve over the same window; Section S1.6). The measured-to-predicted ratios
are $0.962$, $0.941$, $0.939$ (row-major, $r=2,\sqrt5,4$), $1.000$, $0.977$,
$0.987$ (Ulam, $r=\sqrt2,2,4$), and $0.975$, $0.962$, $0.948$ (Cantor,
$r=\sqrt2,\sqrt5,4$). The exact-distance pair statistics of all three
embeddings are therefore quantitatively accounted for by the
Hardy--Littlewood pair densities of the gaps realised by each geometry; the
survey of Soundararajan provides the context for these densities
\cite{SOUNDARARAJAN-2007}.

In the Ulam spiral, distance $\sqrt2$ is enhanced ($g_2=2.057\pm0.021$),
because spiral diagonal neighbours carry small even gaps ($4,6,8,\dots$) whose
pair densities exceed the null; distances $2$ and $4$ are enhanced to
$1.670$ and $1.683$ through the corresponding gap classes. In the Cantor
embedding, distance $\sqrt2$ is at the null level ($g_2=1.008\pm0.021$),
because consecutive integers are diagonal neighbours only within the first
$W$ diagonal blocks of the swept grid, while the long jumps at block
boundaries carry large gaps; distance $2$ is almost completely suppressed
($g_2=0.010$), because the offsets $(\pm2,0),(0,\pm2)$ correspond to odd gaps
$2s+3$, which admit almost no prime pairs, and distance $4$ is enhanced
($g_2=1.931\pm0.021$), in agreement with the gap classes $4s+10$ of the
sweep. Each embedding thus translates a different set of arithmetic gap
constraints into lattice distances, which is precisely why a single scalar
spacing summary is embedding-dependent.

