\subsection{Comparison with standard spatial diagnostics}

A natural criticism of any specialised statistic is that it may add nothing to
established spatial diagnostics. Table~\ref{tab:descriptors} therefore reports,
for the three prime embeddings and the null, the Brody parameter alongside the
Clark--Evans index, Ripley's $K$ function, the pair correlation annulus, and
the two $D_2$ estimators. Three observations follow. First, among the local
descriptors the Brody parameter has by far the largest dynamic range: it
separates all three embeddings in both direction and magnitude; no null
realisation among $1\,000$ exceeded $\beta=1.53$, whereas the row-major value
is $2.153$. Second, descriptors computed from the same
nearest-neighbour sample need not agree. The Clark--Evans index orders the
embeddings as $\beta$ does but compresses the contrast ($1.223\,[1.214,1.231]$,
$1.153\,[1.145,1.162]$, $1.107\,[1.097,1.116]$ against null
$1.076\pm0.005$) and places the Cantor pattern
\emph{above} the null, because it uses only the mean spacing whereas $\beta$
responds to the full shape of the spacing distribution. Third, Ripley's $K$
is informative only at short scales: $L(r)-r$ evaluated at the mean
nearest-neighbour scale reproduces the embedding ordering ($-0.894$, $-0.241$,
$-0.184$ against null $-0.139\pm0.020$), whereas the maximal deviation over
$r\in[1,W/3]$ separates only the Cantor pattern ($9.29$ against null
$8.93\pm0.11$). The annulus pair correlation $g_2$ over $1\le r<3$ px gives
$1.043$, $0.896$, $0.864$ against $0.947\pm0.012$, a weaker and partially
inverted signal that mixes the distance-resolved effects of
Table~\ref{tab:g2}.

