\subsection{Multiscale estimators provide no additional discrimination}

The multiscale picture is negative and is reported as such. The corrected
box-counting slopes over $\varepsilon\in\{2,\dots,128\}$ px are $1.910$,
$1.898$, $1.885$ for the three embeddings, against $1.876\pm0.002$ for the
null: the excess mirrors the local ordering but is confined to the smallest
boxes ($\varepsilon\le8$), while for $\varepsilon\ge8$ px all patterns give
slopes near $2.13$, close to the two-dimensional value. The classical
Grassberger--Procaccia pairwise correlation sum gives $D_2^{\rm GP}=1.993$,
$1.955$, $2.000$ for the three embeddings, against $1.955\pm0.002$ for the
null: there is no consistent embedding ordering, and the Ulam value coincides
with the null. Consequently, no robust multiscale signature of the prime
sequence is detected by either estimator at the scales tested, and the
``correlation dimension'' is not invoked as a discriminating descriptor.
Figure~\ref{fig:d2-validation} shows the two estimators directly; the
finite-sample caution is exactly the one documented by
Theiler~\cite{THEILER-1986} and by Islam and
Sivakumar~\cite{ISLAM-SIVAKUMAR-2002}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_d2_validation.pdf}
  \caption{Multiscale validation. (a) Box-counting partition function
           $\log Z_2$ against box size, with fitted slopes for the three
           embeddings and the null mean. (b) Grassberger--Procaccia pairwise
           slopes over $r\in[2,105]$ px for the three embeddings and the null;
           all values lie close to $2$, and no embedding ordering is
           reproduced.}
  \label{fig:d2-validation}
\end{figure}

%=====================================================================
\section{Discussion}
%=====================================================================

The audited results support a limited but robust conclusion. The fitted Brody
parameter of the nearest-neighbour spacing distribution is strongly
embedding-dependent: $2.153$, $1.856$, and $1.413$ for row-major, Ulam, and
Cantor embeddings of the same $9\,580$ primes in the same window, against
$1.466\pm0.021$ for the density-matched lattice Bernoulli null. The ordering
survives all boundary treatments tested, and it is not reproduced by an
inhomogeneous null with the prime-number-theorem density gradient. The
sparse-integer control likewise fails to reproduce the row-major value.

The Cantor result is particularly informative in this respect. Had only the
row-major embedding been examined, it would have been natural to conclude that
the prime sequence has a strong and intrinsic 2D local-spacing signature. This
conclusion is contradicted by the Cantor map. The observed short-range
structure is a property of the pair (sequence, embedding), not of the sequence
alone.

The exact-distance pair correlations give the clearest account of why. Each
embedding translates a specific set of arithmetic gap constraints into
lattice distances; the measured pair counts are quantitatively predicted by
the Hardy--Littlewood pair densities of the gap classes realised by each
embedding (Section S1.6), and for the row-major embedding the horizontal pair
correlation at distance $2$ equals $1.337\pm0.030$, in quantitative agreement
with the Hardy--Littlewood twin-prime constant $2C_2\approx1.3203$. The
relationship between these distance-resolved effects and the scalar fit was
tested directly by ablation: removal of all twin primes or of all cousin
primes leaves the fitted parameter essentially unchanged ($2.153\to2.155$ and
$2.149$), and removal of both reduces it to $2.090$, while the density-matched
null moves comparably. The scalar Brody fit is therefore not a proxy for the
$r=2$ or $r=4$ pair excesses alone; it aggregates the full short-range
structure of the spacing distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:ablation},
Section S1.7).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_ablation.pdf}
  \caption{Ablation test: fitted Brody parameter after removal of all twin
           primes, all cousin primes, or both, each compared with its
           density-matched lattice Bernoulli null (mean and 95\% band over
           $1\,000$ realisations). The excess over the null is preserved in
           all cases.}
  \label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure}

Several limitations should be kept explicit. First, the Brody family is used
only as an empirical one-parameter fit; no claim of universality is made, and
no claim is made about the fractal-determined Brody values discussed by Sakhr
and Nieminen for random points on fractals~\cite{SAKHR-NIEMINEN-2005}.
Second, only three deterministic embeddings are tested, so the observed
ordering should not be read as exhaustive. Third, the null is the
density-matched lattice Bernoulli field, whose fitted $\beta$ exceeds the
continuous CSR value $\beta=1$ because of lattice discreteness; comparisons
are therefore always made against the empirical lattice null rather than
against the continuous Rayleigh law. Fourth, the multiscale estimators used
here provide no additional discrimination; this negative result is stated
explicitly so that the contribution of the paper is not overstated.

%=====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
%=====================================================================

Two-dimensional binary prime patterns do not possess a unique local spatial
signature independent of embedding. The fitted Brody parameter changes from
$2.153$ for row-major order to $1.856$ for the Ulam spiral and $1.413$ for
Cantor pairing, while a density-matched lattice Bernoulli null yields
$1.466\pm0.021$. The elevated row-major value is shown by the sparse-integer
control and by the inhomogeneous prime-number-theorem null not to be explained
by sparsity or by the density gradient, and absolute values are shown by
density-thinning to be density-dependent even when the prime signal remains
separated from the null. Exact-distance pair correlations of the row-major
embedding reproduce the Hardy--Littlewood twin-prime excess
$2C_2\approx1.3203$ at distance $2$, and the pair counts of all three
embeddings are quantitatively predicted by the Hardy--Littlewood gap
densities of their realised gap classes. Multiscale estimators, by contrast,
are found to provide no additional discrimination between embeddings at the
scales tested. The
appropriate conclusion is not that one descriptor is universally superior,
but that 2D statistical characterisations of arithmetic sequences are
observation-dependent: they depend on what embedding is used and on which
scale-sensitive descriptor is applied.

