\section*{Supplementary Information}

\subsection*{S1. Estimators and null ensembles}

\textbf{S1.1 Brody fitting.}
The Brody parameter is fitted by maximum likelihood on unit-mean
nearest-neighbour spacings, with $3\,000$ bootstrap resamples for the
deterministic patterns. Null distributions use $1\,000$ independent
realisations per null model; ensemble quantiles are reported directly, so no
bootstrap is needed for the nulls. Because nearest-neighbour spacings of a
point pattern are correlated, the bootstrap intervals of the deterministic
patterns are understood as descriptive; inferential statements in the main
text rely on the empirical null ensembles (e.g., no null realisation among
$1\,000$ exceeded $\beta=1.53$).

\textbf{S1.2 Boundary treatments.}
The toroidal nearest-neighbour distance uses the nine periodic images of the
window and excludes self-pairs. The interior-reference treatment retains only
reference points at least $5$ px from the boundary, with Euclidean distances
to all occupied cells. All three treatments are applied identically to prime
patterns and nulls; the interior-reference Bernoulli null yields
$\beta=1.489\pm0.020$ ($1\,000$ realisations).

\textbf{S1.3 Exact-distance pair correlations.}
Equation~\eqref{eq:g2exact} is evaluated by cyclic cross-correlation on the
torus, with the inner sum counting each unordered pair exactly once. The
shift representatives are $(1,0),(0,1)$ for $r=1$; $(1,1),(1,-1)$ for
$r=\sqrt2$; $(2,0),(0,2)$ for $r=2$; $(1,2),(1,-2),(2,1),(2,-1)$ for
$r=\sqrt5$; and $(4,0),(0,4)$ for $r=4$; with $m_d=4,4,4,8,4$,
respectively. For the lattice Bernoulli null the expectation is exactly $1$
for every distance; over $2\,000$ realisations the estimated means and
standard deviations are $0.9999\pm0.021$, $0.9994\pm0.021$,
$1.0002\pm0.021$, $0.9992\pm0.015$, $1.0000\pm0.021$ at
$r=1,\sqrt2,2,\sqrt5,4$, respectively. The direction-resolved statistic used
in Section 3.3 is the single-shift count normalised by $N\rho$, with null
expectation $1$ and null standard deviation $\pm0.030$ per direction.

\textbf{S1.4 Ripley's $K$ and $D_2$ estimators.}
Ripley's $K$ uses the isotropic border correction (fraction of the circle
circumference inside the window, 64 angular samples) on 18 log-spaced radii
$r\in[1,W/3]$ px; deterministic surfaces use 18 bins and null realisations use
the same grid. The box-counting partition function is normalised by the total
mass of the cropped region at each box size; slopes are reported over
$\varepsilon\in\{2,\dots,128\}$ px together with the subrange
$\varepsilon\ge8$ px. The Grassberger--Procaccia pairwise correlation sum is
computed exactly via KD-tree pair counting over $r\in[2,105]$ px. Standard
implementations of the distance-based diagnostics are available in the spatstat
software~\cite{BADDELEY-TURNER-2005}.

\textbf{S1.5 Inhomogeneous null calibration.}
For cell value $n\ge3$ the occupation probability is $p(n)=c/\ln n$, with
$p(1)=p(2)=0$ and $c$ chosen so that the expected occupancy equals the prime
count $9\,580$. The calibration constant is
$c=9\,580/\sum_{n=3}^{99856}1/\ln n\approx1.048$.

\textbf{S1.6 Gap classes and Hardy--Littlewood predictions.}
For each embedding and each shift class of Section S1.3, the multiset of
integer gaps realised by that shift was enumerated over all cells of the
window, and the expected pair count was computed as
$\sum_g m_g\,\pi_g(x)/(x-g)$, where $m_g$ is the multiplicity of gap $g$ and
$\pi_g(x)$ is the exact number of prime pairs with difference $g$ below
$x=99\,856$, obtained by sieve. Table~\ref{tab:hl} lists the measured pair
counts and the predictions. Ratios near unity show that the exact-distance
pair statistics of the prime patterns are accounted for by the
Hardy--Littlewood pair densities~\cite{HARDY-LITTLEWOOD-1923} of the gap
classes realised by each embedding, with the largest deviations confined to
wrap cells at the window boundary (e.g., row-major $r=1$, ratio $0.869$).

