\textbf{S1.7 Twin/cousin ablation.}
All twin primes (the larger member of each pair $(p,p+2)$, $p\le99\,856$) were
removed from the prime set and the row-major fit was recomputed; the same was
done for cousin pairs $(p,p+4)$ and for both families simultaneously, each
time with a density-matched Bernoulli null. The fitted values are $2.155$,
$2.149$, and $2.090$, against nulls $1.408\pm0.022$, $1.407\pm0.023$, and
$1.350\pm0.024$; the excess over the null is preserved in all cases
($\Delta\beta=0.747$, $0.742$, $0.741$). The scalar fit is therefore
insensitive to the twin and cousin pair excesses and aggregates the full
short-range spacing structure.

\subsection*{S2. Finite-size behaviour}

The fitted Brody parameter was recomputed for row-major prime embeddings in
square windows $W^2$ with $W=31,54,100,173,316,547,1000$, each with a
matched lattice Bernoulli null ($1\,000$ realisations for $W\le316$, $200$
for larger windows). Table~\ref{tab:finite} lists $\beta_{\rm prime}$,
$\beta_{\rm null}$, and $\Delta\beta=\beta_{\rm prime}-\beta_{\rm null}$.
The excess $\Delta\beta$ is positive at all sizes except $W=54$, where it is
zero within uncertainty (a small-sample fluctuation of the $421$-prime
pattern), and it is non-monotonic; no convergence or
asymptotic persistence is claimed. Figure~\ref{fig:finite-size} shows the
series.

